Scott's Proposition 7.4 says that the least fixed-point operator is uniquely determined by (i) \(\mathrm{fix}_{\mathbf{A}} : (\mathbf{A} \to \mathbf{A}) \to \mathbf{A}\) for every system \(\mathbf{A}\), (ii) \(\mathrm{fix}(f) = f(\mathrm{fix}(f))\), and (iii) \(h(\mathrm{fix}_{\mathbf{A}}(f)) = \mathrm{fix}_{\mathbf{B}}(g)\) whenever \(h \circ f = g \circ h\) and \(h(\bot_{\mathbf{A}}) = \bot_{\mathbf{B}}\). `proposition_7_4_ii` is (ii), cited as the fixed-point equation of the least fixed point. `proposition_7_4_iii` is (iii) for this operator: `toElement_fix_le_fixElement` places the image of \(\mathrm{fix}\, f\) under a strict commuting \(h\) below \(\mathrm{fix}\, g\), by taking a token back to a stage of the fixed-point chain and applying `fixChain_toElement_le_fix`, whose zero stage is the bottom element by `closure_empty_eq_bot` in `Basic` and whose successor uses commutation, monotonicity of \(h\) and \(g\), and the fixed-point equation; `fixElement_le_toElement_fix` places \(\mathrm{fix}\, g\) below that image because `toElement_comp_fix_le` shows the image is a pre-fixed point of \(g\); antisymmetry is the equality. `proposition_7_4_unique` states that an element fixed by an endomap and lying below every pre-fixed point equals the least fixed point, and its gloss identifies that leastness with Theorem 7.3. `theorem_74` is the conjunction of (ii), (iii), and this element-uniqueness. Condition (i), approximability of an operator of type \((\mathbf{A} \to \mathbf{A}) \to \mathbf{A}\), is not stated, and no argument shows that an arbitrary operator obeying (i)–(iii) equals \(\mathrm{fix}\). The remarks after the proposition — strict maps \(f : \mathbf{A} \to_{\blacksquare} \mathbf{B}\), the extra consistency clause added to Definition 7.1, the operator \(\mathrm{strict}\), the retract of the full function space, and \(\mathbf{A} \times \mathbf{A} \cong (\mathbf{BOOL} \to_s \mathbf{A})\) via \(\mathrm{cond}\) — are not stated. `idMap_botElement` records only that the identity sends bottom to bottom. **Theorem:** partial — (ii) and (iii) hold for the least fixed point, and `proposition_7_4_unique` is leastness of one fixed point among pre-fixed points. The note's claim is that any operator satisfying (i)–(iii) equals \(\mathrm{fix}\). **Proof:** partial — the chain induction proves naturality of this least fixed point, and there is no comparison with another operator satisfying the three equations.
